Looking at this problem:

- The product of up to 10⁴ numbers (each up to 10⁴) can have ~35,000 digits, so we need exact big-integer arithmetic (floating-point logs risk precision errors on leading digits).
- Trailing zero count `C = min(count of factor 2, count of factor 5)`.
- The zero-stripped product is `P' = (product of i with all 2s and 5s stripped) × 2^(cnt2−C) × 5^(cnt5−C)`. Note one of the exponents is 0, and `P'` is never divisible by 10.
- I build `P'` exactly as a `BigInt` (multiplying by small stripped values is O(size) each, so total work is fine for ~33k digits), then convert to string once:
  - If digit count `d ≤ 10`, return the full string + `eC`.
  - Otherwise return first 5 digits + `"..."` + last 5 digits + `eC`.

Verified against all three examples (e.g., [371,375] → stripped product 371·93·373·187·3 = 7219856259, C=3 → "7219856259e3").

```typescript
function abbreviateProduct(left: number, right: number): string {
    let rest: bigint = 1n;
    let cnt2 = 0;
    let cnt5 = 0;
    for (let i = left; i <= right; i++) {
        let x = i;
        while (x % 2 === 0) {
            x = x / 2;
            cnt2++;
        }
        while (x % 5 === 0) {
            x = x / 5;
            cnt5++;
        }
        if (x > 1) {
            rest *= BigInt(x);
        }
    }
    const c = Math.min(cnt2, cnt5);
    let e2 = cnt2 - c;
    let e5 = cnt5 - c;
    while (e2 >= 50) {
        rest *= 1125899906842624n; // 2^50
        e2 -= 50;
    }
    if (e2 > 0) rest *= BigInt(2 ** e2);
    while (e5 >= 22) {
        rest *= 2384185791015625n; // 5^22
        e5 -= 22;
    }
    if (e5 > 0) rest *= BigInt(5 ** e5);
    const s = rest.toString();
    const d = s.length;
    if (d <= 10) {
        return s + 'e' + c;
    }
    return s.slice(0, 5) + '...' + s.slice(d - 5) + 'e' + c;
}
