The problem: find eventual safe states. Classic approach: reverse graph + topological sort (Kahn's algorithm), or DFS with coloring (cycle detection).

Approach 1: DFS with three colors:
- 0 = unvisited, 1 = visiting (in stack), 2 = safe
- If a node leads to a cycle, it's unsafe.

Approach 2: Reverse graph topological sort:
- Reverse all edges. Terminal nodes have out-degree 0 in original graph, so in-degree 0 in reversed graph.
- Use queue, process nodes with in-degree 0 in reversed graph, decrement in-degrees of their neighbors (which are original predecessors).

Let me implement the reverse topological sort approach:

```typescript
function eventualSafeNodes(graph: number[][]): number[] {
    const n = graph.length;
    const reverseGraph: number[][] = Array.from({ length: n }, () => []);
    const outDegree = new Array(n).fill(0);

    for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        outDegree[i] = graph[i].length;
        for (const j of graph[i]) {
            reverseGraph[j].push(i);
        }
    }

    const queue: number[] = [];
    for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        if (outDegree[i] === 0) queue.push(i);
    }

    const safe = new Array(n).fill(false);
    while (queue.length > 0) {
        const node = queue.shift()!;
        safe[node] = true;
        for (const prev of reverseGraph[node]) {
            outDegree[prev]--;
            if (outDegree[prev] === 0) queue.push(prev);
        }
    }

    const result: number[] = [];
    for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        if (safe[i]) result.push(i);
    }
    return result;
}
```

Using shift() on queue could be O(n^2) in worst case for large n (10^4), but that's fine actually — 10^4 shift operations each O(n) would be 10^8... hmm, that could be slow. Better to use an index pointer.

