- `freqCount[f]` — how many distinct values have frequency `f`
- `maxFreq` — maximum frequency (counts only grow, so it's always achieved)

A prefix of length `len` is valid iff one of these holds:
1. `maxFreq === 1` — all values appear once; remove any element.
2. `freqCount[maxFreq] * maxFreq === len - 1` — exactly one leftover element (which must have frequency 1) plus values with frequency `maxFreq`; remove the singleton.
3. `freqCount[maxFreq] === 1 && (freqCount[maxFreq-1] + 1) * (maxFreq - 1) === len - 1` — one value has frequency `maxFreq`, all others have `maxFreq - 1`; remove one occurrence of the max-frequency value (this also covers a single distinct value, since `freqCount[maxFreq-1]` may be 0).

The sum arguments guarantee no false positives: in case 2 the leftover budget of 1 element forces exactly one value of frequency 1; in case 3 the equation forces all non-max values to have frequency exactly `maxFreq - 1`.

