```typescript
function maxSumTwoNoOverlap(nums: number[], firstLen: number, secondLen: number): number {
    const n = nums.length;
    const prefix: number[] = new Array(n + 1).fill(0);
    for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        prefix[i + 1] = prefix[i] + nums[i];
    }

    const sumOf = (start: number, len: number): number => prefix[start + len] - prefix[start];

    // Returns the best total where a subarray of length lenA occurs
    // entirely before a subarray of length lenB.
    const helper = (lenA: number, lenB: number): number => {
        let best = -Infinity;
        let maxA = -Infinity; // best sum of a lenA subarray ending before current lenB subarray
        for (let i = lenA; i + lenB <= n; i++) {
            // lenA subarray ending at index i-1 (i.e., starting at i - lenA)
            maxA = Math.max(maxA, sumOf(i - lenA, lenA));
            best = Math.max(best, maxA + sumOf(i, lenB));
        }
        return best;
    };

    return Math.max(helper(firstLen, secondLen), helper(secondLen, firstLen));
}
```

Looking at this problem:

**Key insights:**
1. Sort gardens ascending. In any optimal solution, the completed gardens can be assumed to be the **largest** ones (completing a garden with more flowers costs less, and the incomplete set being the smallest gardens is cheapest to raise its minimum).
2. Enumerate `k` = number of complete gardens (the top `k` after sorting). Cost `C(k) = suf[n-k]` (suffix deficit to `target`). If affordable, use the remaining budget to maximize the minimum of the first `m = n-k` gardens, capped at `target - 1` (raising to `target` would make them complete — that case is covered by a larger `k`).
3. Gardens already `>= target` are *always* complete, so `k` must be at least `n - lo` where `lo` = count of gardens below target (otherwise the "incomplete minimum" would be phantom and could overcount, e.g. `flowers=[7], target=5, full=1, partial=1000` must answer `1`, not `4000`).
4. For fixed `m` and budget, binary search the max achievable minimum `v ∈ [f[0], target-1]`; raising cost is `v·p − pre[p]` where `p` = count of the first `m` gardens below `v` (found in O(1) via a precomputed lower-bound table).
5. Also consider `k = n` (all complete → beauty `n·full`).

Since `C(k)` is non-decreasing in `k`, we can break early. I verified this against both examples (14 and 30) and several edge cases (all-complete inputs, tight budgets, degenerate boundaries).

```typescript
function maximumBeauty(flowers: number[], newFlowers: number, target: number, full: number, partial: number): number {
    const n = flowers.length;
    const f = flowers.slice().sort((a, b) => a - b);

    // prefix sums of sorted values
    const pre: number[] = new Array(n + 1).fill(0);
    for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) pre[i + 1] = pre[i] + f[i];

    // suf[i] = flowers needed to bring gardens i..n-1 up to target
    const suf: number[] = new Array(n + 1).fill(0);
    for (let i = n - 1; i >= 0; i--) {
        const need = target - f[i];
        suf[i] = suf[i + 1] + (need > 0 ? need : 0);
    }

    // gardens with value < target occupy positions [0, lo)
    let lo = 0;
    while (lo < n && f[lo] < target) lo++;

    // lbArr[v] = number of gardens with value < v (for v in [0, target-1])
    const lbArr = new Int32Array(target);
    let ptr = 0;
    for (let v = 0; v < target; v++) {
        while (ptr < n && f[ptr] < v) ptr++;
        lbArr[v] = ptr;
    }

    // cost to raise the minimum of the first m gardens to value v
    const raiseCost = (m: number, v: number): number => {
        const p = Math.min(lbArr[v], m);
        return v * p - pre[p];
    };

    let ans = 0;

    // k = number of complete gardens (the k largest ones); k >= n - lo is required,
    // since gardens already >= target are always complete
    for (let k = n - lo; k <= n; k++) {
        const cost = suf[n - k];
        if (cost > newFlowers) break; // completing more gardens only costs more
        if (k === n) {
            // all gardens complete -> no incomplete minimum
            if (n * full > ans) ans = n * full;
            continue;
        }
        const m = n - k;
        const budget = newFlowers - cost;
        // maximize the minimum of the first m gardens, capped at target - 1
        let l = f[0];
        let r = target - 1;
        while (l < r) {
            const mid = (l + r + 1) >> 1;
            if (raiseCost(m, mid) <= budget) l = mid;
            else r = mid - 1;
        }
        const cand = k * full + l * partial;
        if (cand > ans) ans = cand;
    }

    return ans;
}
```

**Complexity:** Sorting `O(n log n)`; building the lower-bound table `O(n + target)`; the main loop runs at most `n+1` times with an `O(log target)` binary search using `O(1)` cost queries → `O(n log target)` overall. All intermediate values fit safely in JavaScript numbers (max ≈ 2×10¹⁰ < 2⁵³).

