This is the classic "Next Greater Element II" problem. The standard approach is to use a monotonic stack and iterate over the array twice (2n iterations) to handle the circular nature.

Approach:
- Create result array initialized with -1.
- Use a stack of indices.
- Iterate i from 0 to 2n-1, using i % n as the actual index.
- While stack is not empty and nums[stack top] < nums[i % n], pop and set result[popped] = nums[i % n].
- Push i % n (only if i < n, or always push — pushing always works too but pushing only when i < n avoids duplicates; actually pushing always is fine but unnecessary; standard is to push only when i < n to keep stack size bounded, but pushing always also works since we pop when we find greater. Actually if we push always, stack could grow to 2n which is fine memory-wise. But cleaner to push only when i < n).

Let me write:

```typescript
function nextGreaterElements(nums: number[]): number[] {
    const n = nums.length;
    const result = new Array(n).fill(-1);
    const stack: number[] = [];
    for (let i = 0; i < 2 * n; i++) {
        const idx = i % n;
        while (stack.length > 0 && nums[stack[stack.length - 1]] < nums[idx]) {
            const top = stack.pop()!;
            result[top] = nums[idx];
        }
        if (i < n) stack.push(idx);
    }
    return result;
}
```

This is O(n) time and space. Correct.

