This is the classic "Scramble String" problem (LeetCode 87). The standard solution is a recursive approach with memoization.

The idea: two strings s1 and s2 are scrambles of each other if:
1. They are equal, OR
2. There exists a split point i such that either:
   - s1[0..i) is a scramble of s2[0..i) AND s1[i..n) is a scramble of s2[i..n) (no swap), OR
   - s1[0..i) is a scramble of s2[n-i..n) AND s1[i..n) is a scramble of s2[0..n-i) (swap).

Pruning: check character frequency (anagram check) first — if the character counts don't match, return false immediately.

With memoization using a Map keyed by `s1 + '#' + s2` or by indices.

Let me write the solution:

```typescript
function isScramble(s1: string, s2: string): boolean {
    const n = s1.length;
    if (n !== s2.length) return false;

    const memo = new Map<string, boolean>();

    function helper(a: string, b: string): boolean {
        if (a === b) return true;
        // frequency check
        const count = new Array(26).fill(0);
        for (let i = 0; i < a.length; i++) {
            count[a.charCodeAt(i) - 97]++;
            count[b.charCodeAt(i) - 97]--;
        }
        for (let c of count) {
            if (c !== 0) return false;
        }

        const key = a + '#' + b;
        if (memo.has(key)) return memo.get(key)!;

        const len = a.length;
        for (let i = 1; i < len; i++) {
            // no swap
            if (helper(a.substring(0, i), b.substring(0, i)) && 
                helper(a.substring(i), b.substring(i))) {
                memo.set(key, true);
                return true;
            }
            // swap
            if (helper(a.substring(0, i), b.substring(len - i)) && 
                helper(a.substring(i), b.substring(0, len - i))) {
                memo.set(key, true);
                return true;
            }
        }

        memo.set(key, false);
        return false;
    }

    return helper(s1, s2);
}
```

